  "description": "Remove measured allocation and redundant message traversal costs from CPU min-sum LDPC updates while preserving the decoder's numerical contract.\n\n## Background\n\nLlr::boxplus_minsum_n currently collects a temporary Vec<f32> for accelerated calls. LdpcDecoder reconstructs all other messages for each outgoing edge and searches neighbor positions. The implementation should follow measured hot-path evidence for min-sum, normalized min-sum and offset min-sum, with reusable primitives in the owning library layer.\n\n## Success Criteria\n\n- [hard] REQ-01: Satisfy the [Zen 3 measurement contract](dev/active/1a379447-zen3-cpu-performance/measurement-contract.md) for correctness, reproducible release benchmarks, comparison validity and adoption; receipts pin its committed version, the frozen protocol and the cell addendum. Include mandatory before/after evidence for production changes and preserve negative outcomes.\n\n- [hard] REQ-07: Demonstrate with an allocation counter that repeated decoding with prepared workspace and a fixed supported configuration performs zero heap allocations in steady state. Initial construction and documented workspace growth are measured separately.\n\n- [hard] REQ-08: Use one canonical precomputed edge indexing/layout and a shared minimum, second-minimum and sign reduction so each check update visits incoming messages a bounded number of times linear in check degree, without per-output neighbor searches or repeated exclusion-buffer construction.\n\n- [hard] REQ-09: Preserve the supported scalar reference's observable behavior for ties, signed zero, finite extrema, empty/degree-one cases where supported, clipping and non-finite inputs under the declared API contract. Preserve arithmetic order where it is part of that contract; do not hide numerical changes in a performance refactor.\n\n- [hard] REQ-10: Publish matched AFF3CT comparisons for check-node updates, full iterations and full decoding on the frozen DVB-T2 and NR workloads. Include single-core and physical-core/SMT scaling, allocation counts, representative degree distributions and iteration/early-exit behavior.\n\n\n\n\n## Notes\n\nSource references: [Cassagne2019].",
